{
 "id": "2018-grebenkina-building",
 "name": "Building Facade Aesthetic Ratings",
 "description": "Faithful replication of Experiment 3 of Grebenkina, Brachmann, Bertamini, Kaduhm and Redies (2018), Frontiers in Neuroscience, \"Edge-orientation entropy predicts preference for diverse types of man-made images\", with the authors' 50 CC BY facade photographs (OSF cxyj4). The greyscale photographs, mostly from Vienna and Berlin, show two storeys of a building and range from plain window grids to richly ornamented fronts. Participants rate all 50 facades in three blocks (pleasing, interesting, harmonious), block order and image order random. Each trial starts with a 500 ms fixation cross, then the facade on a black screen with a bar below it; one click on the bar gives a rating from 1 (not …) to 100 and moves to the next facade, as in the original. In the original, 27 participants' ratings were predicted to about 50% by the entropy of edge orientations (more evenly spread edge orientations, higher pleasing and interesting ratings). Image keys 001–050 match the authors' ratings file (Facades_ratings_variables.xlsx, which also lists edge-orientation entropy, edge density and self-similarity per image; divide our 1–100 values by 100 to compare). About 15 minutes. Differences from the original study: (1) online instead of a lab with chin rest and calibrated monitor, so image size varies with the screen; (2) the 16-bit TIFF images are converted to 8-bit JPEG; (3) the questionnaire asks only sex, age and formal training in art, architecture or design (the original also asked handedness, vision and art/music interest); (4) keyboard users can rate with the arrow keys and Enter.",
